answer.en:
Generic interfaces describe a contract that works with different types while preserving type safety. This is done by adding type parameters `<T>` (or multiple ones).

#### Example: basic generic interface

```typescript
interface Box<T> {
  value: T;
}

const numBox: Box<number> = { value: 42 };
const strBox: Box<string> = { value: "Hello" };
```

#### With multiple parameters

```typescript
interface Pair<K, V> {
  key: K;
  value: V;
}

const pair: Pair<string, number> = { key: "age", value: 30 };
```

#### Generic interfaces with functions

```typescript
interface Repository<T> {
  getAll(): T[];
  getById(id: number): T | null;
}

class UserRepo implements Repository<{ id: number; name: string }> {
  private users = [{ id: 1, name: "Alice" }];
  getAll() { return this.users; }
  getById(id: number) { return this.users.find(u => u.id === id) ?? null; }
}
```

#### Why use them:

- build reusable APIs.
- preserve relationships between types.
- avoid code duplication.

