answer.en:
In TypeScript, classes can be parameterized by adding a type parameter `<T>` after the class name. This enables reusable classes while preserving type safety.

#### Basic generic class example

```typescript
class Box<T> {
  constructor(public content: T) {}
  getContent(): T {
    return this.content;
  }
}

const numberBox = new Box<number>(123);
const stringBox = new Box<string>("Hello");
```

#### Class with multiple type parameters

```typescript
class Pair<K, V> {
  constructor(public key: K, public value: V) {}
}

const pair = new Pair<string, number>("id", 42);
```

#### Generic constraints using extends

```typescript
class Collection<T extends { id: number }> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  add(item: T) { this.items.push(item); }
  getById(id: number): T | undefined {
    return this.items.find(i => i.id === id);
  }
}

const users = new Collection<{ id: number; name: string }>();
users.add({ id: 1, name: "Alice" });
```

#### Benefits:

- Reusable classes with type safety.
- Shared logic across different types.
- Strong coupling between methods and properties.

