answer.en:
In TypeScript, generic parameters can be constrained using extends to require compatibility with a specific type or interface. This allows safe access to properties or methods inside functions and classes.

#### Function example

```typescript
interface HasLength {
  length: number;
}

function logLength<T extends HasLength>(item: T): void {
  console.log(item.length);
}

logLength("Hello");
logLength([1, 2, 3]);
logLength(42);
```

#### Class example

```typescript
class Collection<T extends { id: number }> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  add(item: T) { this.items.push(item); }
  getById(id: number): T | undefined {
    return this.items.find(i => i.id === id);
  }
}

const users = new Collection<{ id: number; name: string }>();
users.add({ id: 1, name: "Alice" });
```

#### Benefits:

- Safe access to required properties and methods.
- Keeps generics flexible but constrained to valid types.

